Ladybird sizes a ratio-only replaced element from definite available
inline space when measuring its fit-content size. Without that space,
its intrinsic contribution starts at zero instead of the viewport
size, with size constraints transferred through the aspect ratio.
Cyclic percentage inline sizes use the default object size instead.

Gecko returns zero for a missing natural inline size in nsImageFrame::
IntrinsicISize. Its nsImageFrame::ComputeSize delegates used sizing to
nsContainerFrame::ComputeSizeWithIntrinsicDimensions, which fills the
containing block for a ratio-only image and subtracts its outer edges.

Blink routes intrinsic contributions through ComputeReplacedSize,
called by ComputeMinAndMaxContentContributionForReplaced. Within
ComputeReplacedSizeInternal, definite inline space provides the
stretch-fit size. Its indefinite-space fallback has zero content size,
except that percentage inline lengths use the default natural size.

WebKit separates intrinsic measurements from used-size calculations in
RenderReplaced::computeReplacedLogicalWidth. A ratio-only element has a
zero intrinsic width subject to transferred bounds. For used sizing,
computeConstrainedLogicalWidth supplies the available width instead.

The zero intrinsic fallback intentionally differs from CSS Sizing 3,
which specifies the corresponding initial-containing-block dimension.
It keeps a ratio-only image from widening a shrink-to-fit container.
